# Onboarding Checklist

## Overview

This skill is designed to help you onboard new engineers to the team. Onboarding is one of the most important processes in any engineering organization, because a good onboarding experience helps new hires become productive quickly and feel welcome. A bad onboarding experience can lead to frustration and confusion. That is why it is important to follow this checklist carefully and completely.

In this skill, you will create accounts, grant repository access, assign a buddy, schedule a setup session, and send the first-week plan. Each of these steps is described in detail below.

## Why onboarding matters

Onboarding matters because new engineers need access to tools, people, and information before they can contribute. Without access, they cannot read code. Without a buddy, they do not know who to ask. Without a setup session, they may spend days configuring their machine. Without a first-week plan, they may not know what to work on. This is why every step in this checklist matters.

## Step-by-step instructions

### Step 1: Create accounts

Create the new hire's email and Slack accounts, and their GitHub account if they don't have one. Add their GitHub account to the `acme/engineering` team. This is important because without accounts the new hire cannot do anything.

### Step 2: Grant repository access

Open an access request in the #it-requests Slack channel with the new hire's GitHub username. This is important because repository access is required to read and change code.

### Step 3: Assign an onboarding buddy

Pair the new hire with an onboarding buddy from their own team. This is important because the buddy answers questions during the first weeks.

### Step 4: Schedule the setup session

Schedule a 30-minute setup session on the new hire's first day, where they run `make bootstrap` with their buddy. This is important because a working development environment is required to contribute.

### Step 5: Send the first-week plan

Send the new hire the first-week plan from `docs/first-week.md`. This is important because the plan tells them what to work on.

## Summary of the steps

To summarize, the steps are:

1. Create accounts and add the new hire to `acme/engineering` on GitHub.
2. Request repository access in #it-requests.
3. Assign an onboarding buddy from their team.
4. Schedule a 30-minute day-one session to run `make bootstrap`.
5. Send `docs/first-week.md`.

## Best practices

- Be friendly and welcoming.
- Be patient with the new hire.
- Make sure nothing is forgotten.
- Communicate clearly.
- Follow up regularly.
- Always follow the checklist.

## Remember

Remember to create accounts, grant repository access, assign a buddy, schedule the setup session, and send the first-week plan. Do not skip any step. Every step is important. If you skip a step, the new hire may have a bad experience.

## Recap

In this skill, you learned how to onboard a new engineer: create accounts, request access in #it-requests, assign a buddy, schedule the 30-minute setup session with `make bootstrap`, and send `docs/first-week.md`. Following this checklist ensures a smooth and successful onboarding experience for every new hire.
